border=3D0>
  <TBODY>
  <TR>
    <TD align=3Dmiddle width=3D2394 height=3D305>
      <P style=3D"MARGIN-TOP: 0px; MARGIN-BOTTOM: 10px" =
align=3Djustify><FONT=20
      face=3DArial color=3D#000080 size=3D4><IMG height=3D157 =
hspace=3D10=20
      =
src=3D"http://www.eurotravelling.net/italy/vatican_city/images/piazza_s_p=
ietro.jpg"=20
      width=3D250 align=3Dleft vspace=3D10 border=3D1>T</FONT><FONT =
face=3DArial=20
      color=3D#000080 size=3D3>he smallest country in the world has a =
rich, if=20
      narrowly focused, history. Religion, art and construction dominate =
the=20
      region that is now the Holy See. Politically, the area followed =
Rome and=20
      Italy until 1929, long before that the region to the northwest of =
central=20
      Rome was inextricably connected to the Catholic Church. In about =
65 AD the=20
      Romans were sacrificing Christians in Nero's Circo Vaticano, and =
it's=20
      probably here that St Peter - regarded as the first Pope - met his =
end.=20
      About 100 years later the site was abandoned and a small monument =
to the=20
      martyr was erected. In 315, Emperor Constantine ordered =
construction of a=20
      basilica on the apostle's tomb. St Peter's Basilica was =
consecrated in=20
      326.</FONT></P>
      <P style=3D"MARGIN-TOP: 10px; MARGIN-BOTTOM: 10px" =
align=3Djustify><FONT=20
      face=3DArial color=3D#000080 size=3D3>The history of the Vatican =
is a history of=20
      power plays, schisms, crusades and inquisitions. For more than 70 =
years in=20
      the 14th century, Rome was pope less. Abandoned because of =
political=20
      instability, the thousand-year-old centre of the Church, founded =
on the=20
      tombs of Saints Peter and Paul, lost not only the prestige of =
being the=20
      home of the papacy, but also the considerable income of tithing=20
      Christendom, reducing Rome to extreme poverty. After the return of =
the=20
      papacy, Rome began to strengthen again. Although Rome is now far =
more=20
      self-sufficient, the Vatican is still hugely important to the =
economic and=20
      cultural security of the city.</FONT></P>
      <P style=3D"MARGIN-TOP: 10px; MARGIN-BOTTOM: 10px" =
align=3Djustify><FONT=20
      face=3DArial color=3D#000080 size=3D3><IMG height=3D155 =
hspace=3D10=20
      =
src=3D"http://www.eurotravelling.net/italy/vatican_city/images/the_bsasil=
ica_di_s_petro.jpg"=20
      width=3D250 align=3Dright vspace=3D10 border=3D1>A thousand years =
after St Peter's=20
      Basilica was built it was - not surprisingly - in a poor state of =
repair.=20
      A new basilica was designed. The demolition of the old one caused =
great=20
      controversy at the time as it involved destroying Byzantine =
mosaics and=20
      frescoes by artists, including Giotto. After 150 years - and with =
the=20
      input of artists like Bramante, Raphael, Michelangelo and Giacomo =
della=20
      Porta, the basilica was completed. Most of the kudos for the =
massive=20
      project rests with Michelangelo, who took it over in 1547, aged =
72, and=20
      designed the 119m (390ft) dome. The cavernous interior can hold an =
amazing=20
      60,000 people. Excavations that commenced in 1940 under the =
basilica=20
      unearthed remnants of the original church, a tomb believed to be =
that of=20
      St Peter, and the bones of an elderly, well-built man. Pope Paul =
VI=20
      declared these bones to be those of St Peter.</FONT></P>
      <P style=3D"MARGIN-TOP: 10px; MARGIN-BOTTOM: 10px" =
align=3Djustify><FONT=20
      face=3DArial color=3D#000080 size=3D3><IMG height=3D137 =
hspace=3D10=20
      =
src=3D"http://www.eurotravelling.net/italy/vatican_city/images/swissguard=
_b.jpg"=20
      width=3D250 align=3Dleft vspace=3D10 border=3D1>In the 1870s, =
during the=20
      occupation of Italy by Napoleon III, the papal soldiers - known as =
the=20
      Swiss Guard - provided the only significant resistance to the =
Italian=20
      forces as they reclaimed Rome. They refused to recognise the =
Kingdom of=20
      Italy, so the Pope was stripped of his temporal powers and evicted =
from=20
      his palace. During the 1920s, Fascist dictator Benito Mussolini =
worked to=20
      restore the independent papal state, and autonomy was announced in =
1929.=20
      The early years of independence saw the establishment and rapid =
expansion=20
      of the Vatican 'society'. A radio station was opened by wireless =
inventor=20
      Marconi in the early 1930s, and the city now has one television =
station=20
      and seven radio stations spreading Good News to anyone who'll=20
      listen.</FONT></P>
      <P style=3D"MARGIN-TOP: 10px; MARGIN-BOTTOM: 0px" =
align=3Djustify><FONT=20
      face=3DArial color=3D#000080 size=3D3><SPAN=20
      style=3D"mso-fareast-font-family: Times New Roman; =
mso-bidi-font-family: Times New Roman; mso-ansi-language: EN-GB; =
mso-fareast-language: EN-US; mso-bidi-language: AR-SA">More=20
      recently, scandals and innuendo relating to the Italian Mafia's =
control=20
      over the Vatican have surfaced. Given their reported control over =
the rest=20
      of Italy - the Mafia's estimated worth is about 12% of Italy's =
gross=20
      national product - there seems no reason to suppose that crime=20
      organizations don't have connections with the Holy See. Rumours =
were=20
      fuelled when Pope John Paul I died suddenly in 1978 after only 25 =
days in=20
      office, having earlier vowed to break the Mafia's hold over the =
people of=20
      Rome.</SPAN></FONT> </P></TD></TR></TBODY></TABLE>
